
Global Operations & Engineering Runbook


Document Metadata

• System/Application: Core Services Portal
• Classification: Internal / Confidential
• Version: 1.0.0
• Last Updated: October 2026

[RUN-01] Database Connection Pool Exhausted


Symptoms & Error Alerts

• Applications throwing FATAL: remaining connection slots are reserved for non-replication superuser connections
• HTTP 500 errors across client applications.
• High latency on database read/write actions.

Underlying Cause

The PostgreSQL database connection pool has hit its max limit because connection instances are not closing down after active API requests finish.

Step-by-Step Resolution Path

1. Log directly into the central monitoring cluster or proxy server.
2. Run the command pg_activity or check active queries using the following SQL terminal execution:sql
SELECT pid, query, state FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE state != 'idle';
Use code with caution.
3. Locate hanging connections that have been active for more than 5 minutes.
4. Safely kill zombie connections by executing:sql
SELECT pg_terminate_backend(pid) FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE state = 'idle' AND query_start < now() - interval '5 minutes';
Use code with caution.
5. If the connection pool fills up immediately again, increase the max connection limit temporarily in postgresql.conf and issue an app rollout restart.

[RUN-02] API Gateway 504 Bad Gateway Error


Symptoms & Error Alerts

• Load balancer health checks failing.
• Users receiving explicit HTTP 504 Gateway Timeout screens.
• Nginx logs throwing upstream timed out (110: Connection timed out) while reading response header from upstream.

Underlying Cause

The front-facing Nginx or AWS ALB gateway is waiting for a response from the internal downstream microservice, but the backend service is stuck or deadlocked.

Step-by-Step Resolution Path

1. Identify which specific route is timing out by running an inspection on the Nginx access logs:bash
tail -n 100 /var/log/nginx/access.log | grep "504"
Use code with caution.
2. Once the failing microservice is isolated (e.g., the billing service), check its active containers or server memory footprint.
3. Perform a rolling restart on that service cluster to clear the memory deadlock:bash
kubectl rollout restart deployment/billing-service -n production
Use code with caution.
4. Confirm gateway traffic health returns to nominal levels by executing a health probe test:bash
curl -I https://yourcompany.com
Use code with caution.

[RUN-03] User Authentication & JWT Failures


Symptoms & Error Alerts

• Users are abruptly logged out of their sessions.
• Login workflows throwing Signature verification failed or 401 Unauthorized codes.
• Auth microservice container logs showing Key fetch failed from JWKS endpoint.

Underlying Cause

The authentication gateway cannot pull the active public signing keys from the identity server, or the local token cache has corrupted entries.

Step-by-Step Resolution Path

1. Navigate to the Redis cache dashboard and check memory thresholds.
2. If the memory is heavily utilized, clear the auth token metadata cache manually:bash
redis-cli -h auth-cache.internal flushdb
Use code with caution.
3. Trigger a manual sync sequence to pull fresh verification keys directly from the central Identity Provider (IdP).
4. Verify signature processing behaves correctly by attempting a mock login transaction using a test user account.

[RUN-04] Disk Space Alert - Log Rotation Fix


Symptoms & Error Alerts

• Server monitoring systems generating high-priority Disk Space Critical: /var/log usage > 95% alerts.
• Core system processes dropping data packets because they cannot execute file-write operations.

Underlying Cause

The system log-rotation utility (logrotate) has hung up or crashed, allowing application debug logs to build up indefinitely on the server.

Step-by-Step Resolution Path

1. Securely SSH into the impacted host server machine.
2. Verify exactly which directories are consuming disk space by executing:bash
df -h && du -sh /var/log/* | sort -rh | head -5
Use code with caution.
3. If massive .log files are found, compress or clear the contents of inactive old logs. Do not delete active file descriptors. Use truncation:bash
truncate -s 0 /var/log/nginx/error.log.1
Use code with caution.
4. Restart the log rotation service daemon to ensure the automation schedule processes upcoming files properly:bash
systemctl restart logrotate